body:
### What problem are you trying to solve?

`MutationObserver` is chunk-aligned rather than parser-aligned: when the HTML parser streams markup across network chunks, it inserts an element into the DOM on its opening tag and can yield before its closing tag arrives, flushing half-built subtrees to `MutationObserver` callbacks at arbitrary intermediate microtask checkpoints. By contrast, when DOM is built synchronously in script (`el.append(a); el.append(b);`), `MutationObserver` naturally batches all mutations in the task and delivers them together at the microtask checkpoint.

Components and streaming applications that need complete subtrees—such as custom elements that validate or index light-DOM children (`<s-select>` validating `<s-option>` children), elements that evaluate text/code once, or delegated island hydration—see incomplete children or perform redundant O(N^2) work across chunks.

At the same time, the central objection in WICG/webcomponents#809 to a dedicated one-shot custom element `parsedCallback()` is that it encourages reading `this.children` once at parse end and ignoring subsequent dynamic DOM mutations.

### What solutions exist today?

Because `MutationObserver` isn't aligned with element parse boundaries, server-driven and streaming applications reconstruct parse completion in userland at measurable main-thread and wire cost:

- **Ancestor `nextSibling` walk + per-element `subtree: true` `MutationObserver`** (`WebReflection/html-parsed-element`, `patricknelson/svelte-retag#20`): walks ancestors on every insertion (reported at 100ms+ of main-thread time on medium documents) and leaves last-child elements "unparsed" until `DOMContentLoaded`.
- **Regex over raw HTML chunks + `document.write()` into a detached document / `<iframe>`** (`marko-js/writable-dom`).
- **Sentinel child elements** (`<the-end>`) or **trailing inline `<script>` tags** after each streamed region (extra DOM nodes/wire bytes and requires CSP nonces/hashes or `unsafe-inline`).
- **Deferring `customElements.define()` to `DOMContentLoaded` or `requestAnimationFrame` / `setTimeout`**, which delays upgrades until the whole document finishes or races the parser across chunks.

### How would you solve it?

A synchronous `element.isParsing` getter (#12200), paired with a `parseEndChildList` option on `MutationObserver.observe()` (without delaying DOM insertion itself—the parser still mutates the live DOM immediately).

```webidl
partial interface Element {
  readonly attribute boolean isParsing;
};

partial dictionary MutationObserverInit {
  boolean parseEndChildList = false;
};
```

An element is "parsing" when it is on any HTML parser's stack of open elements (start tag inserted, end tag not yet popped), is the fragment context of an ongoing HTML stream (such as `streamHTML*`), or is the target element (parent of the processing instruction) for an ongoing `<template for>` patch.

With `element.isParsing` alone, a `childList` observer already receives a `childList` record at parse end whenever the final chunk added at least one child—the only gap is when the end tag closes an empty element or arrives in a chunk after the last child. `parseEndChildList: true` closes that gap so parse end always produces a `childList` record:

```js
class CustomSelect extends HTMLElement {
  #mo = new MutationObserver(() => {
    if (!this.isParsing) this.#syncOptions();
  });
  connectedCallback() {
    this.#mo.observe(this, { childList: true, parseEndChildList: true });
    if (!this.isParsing) this.#syncOptions();
  }
}
```

1. **No timing change:** Records are delivered at every microtask checkpoint as today. Nothing is held, and `observe()`, `takeRecords()`, and `disconnect()` behave as today. Multiple targets and mixing `parseEndChildList: true` / `false` (including with a normal `subtree: true` registration) on the same observer are allowed.
2. **Guaranteed `childList` record on parse end:** When an element observed with `parseEndChildList: true` finishes parsing, if a `childList` record for it is already pending (because children arrived in the final chunk), that non-empty record covers parse end; only if none is pending is an empty fallback `childList` record (`addedNodes: []`, `removedNodes: []`) queued. Either way, the observer always gets a callback with a `childList` record after `isParsing` has become `false`.
3. **Target's own `childList` only:** Requires `childList: true`; combining with `subtree: true` throws a `TypeError`, so the parser does not have to report every element it closes.

Unlike a generic `parseEnd: true` name, `parseEndChildList: true` states that parse end is surfaced through `childList` rather than a separate record type. The empty fallback record requires relaxing the assertion in "queue a tree mutation record" that `addedNodes` or `removedNodes` is non-empty (only for `parseEndChildList: true` registrations), and authors check `isParsing` inside the callback and handle already-parsed elements at the `observe()` call site.

**Off-main-thread / tree-op parsers (e.g. Gecko):** An engine that separates parsing from main-thread DOM construction via queued tree operations does not need to emit an "end-parse" operation or flip an `isParsing` bit for every element's opening and closing tags (addressing the parser-to-DOM traffic concern in [w3c/csswg-drafts#12578 (comment)](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/12578#issuecomment-3209690246)). Any element that opens and closes without an intervening parser batch flush (chunk or timer boundary), `<script>`, or custom element reaction can never be observed by script while open—by the time any `MutationObserver` or script can hold a reference to it and read `el.isParsing`, it is already `false` (the default). The parser only needs to mark the $O(\text{stack depth})$ ancestor elements that are actually on the stack of open elements *at* a batch flush, `<script>`, or custom element reaction (and later emit a close op for those few nodes, which can itself be batched up to the next such boundary).

#### Alternative considered: parser-aligned delivery (`alignWithParser()`)

We also considered adding `observer.alignWithParser(target, { signal })` ([Option 1 in the explainer](https://github.com/WICG/declarative-partial-updates/blob/main/parse-end-mutation-observer-explainer.md#option-1)), which holds the entire observer's record queue while any aligned target is parsing and flushes once parsing ends (invoking the callback even with an empty record list if nothing was queued). That avoids both the empty fallback `childList` record and the `isParsing` check inside the callback, and works with `subtree: true`. However, it changes `MutationObserver` microtask delivery timing (holding records across intermediate checkpoints, including inline `<script>`s) and requires a separate observer per independently-completing element, whereas `parseEndChildList: true` preserves existing delivery timing and composes on a single observer.

